A||N||11||Bugsy||Power Lock Actuators||||9||
Z||000000||Bugsy||07-10-2000||10:46 AM||||I got tired of my power lock on the drivers door not working today and tore into the door.<BR>I have the new actuator but I didn't know the original was riveted ....would it be easier to go at the actuator by removing the door skin? any input is appreciated<BR>Kevin||||reg||9
Z||000001||Monkeyman||07-10-2000||11:34 AM||||Yes and no.  Yes, it would be easier with the door skin removed, but it's a b**** to remove the skin.  You'd be better off to leave the skin on.  Drill out the center portion of the rivets.  Then either use a larger drill bit to drill out the outer part of rivet (it'll probably just spin) or use a chisel to knock it out.<P>It's alot easier than it sounds.  I removed 2 of them in about 10 minutes.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000002||286SE'S||07-10-2000||12:01 PM||||I have had no luck with trying to drill the rivets out on the lock actuators. A grinding<BR>wheel seems to work better for me. The drill<BR>bit will not stay in the center of the rivet.<BR>Grinding down the round surface, stopping just before reaching the door metal, then using a drill is much easier. Now if I could only find a cordless grinder to take to the junkyard. Put everything back together with nuts and bolts.<BR>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||07-10-2000||12:24 PM||||Use a punch to drive out the center pin on the rivets.  Just punch them through.  Then it is much easier to keep the drill bit centered on the rivet.  When the rivet starts to spin tilt the drill bit and attack the rivet from an angle.  It will eventually cut through.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||07-10-2000||12:24 PM||||Use a punch to drive out the center pin on the rivets.  Just punch them through.  Then it is much easier to keep the drill bit centered on the rivet.  When the rivet starts to spin tilt the drill bit and attack the rivet from an angle.  It will eventually cut through.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||07-10-2000||12:27 PM||||Sorry for the double post.  I don't know what happened.<P>BTW, the power lock actuators are the same for both driver and passenger doors.  The bracket that is bolted to the actuator is reversible.  Simply unbolt it from the actuator and turn it around.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bugsy||07-10-2000||06:53 PM||||almost sounds like more work than its worth...but i have remote keyless entry..so the holes the rivets go thru are they big enough to run a bolt thru or will it be necessary to get out the rivet gun? TIA<BR>Kevin||||reg||1
Z||000007||TOM||07-10-2000||09:20 PM||||Hey Carrolles that just what I was going to say. When I installed power locks in a friends car I think I drilled the holes out to 1/4 the used bolts and nuts. Or you could use a drive rivet||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||07-10-2000||10:51 PM||||just to cover the obvious, you did check all the rest of the wiring? there are relays in the power lock system, tho I don't think those are your problem. You also want to make sure the wiring in the A post is good. A loose connector there would screwup the lock on only one side. A loose connector can pass enough juice to work a new/unloaded lock motor but not enough to work the motor as installed.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bugsy||07-11-2000||12:47 AM||||oh i forgot to mention, i connected the new actuator, and it works fine....the one still in the door....tries once in a while when i'm playing with the buttons and moving the actuator<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/red_racer.jpg">  <BR>behold an image of my tormenter   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Kevin<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Bugsy (edited 07-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||wkayl||07-11-2000||08:38 PM||||I just replace the actuator on my 86GT.  I just reached in and removed the screws that hold it to the bracket that is rivited.||||reg||1
